{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And they shall fear.</b> Some say it is from the language of יראה (fear) and it is deficient a yod, and some say it is as it sounds [i.e., 'they shall see']:" "And the meaning of <b>the name of YHWH</b>: His deed, and it testifies to His glory." "<b>For He shall come like a river, [the enemy] pressed.</b> Like 'straits and distress have found me' (Psalms 119:143). And behold, it is a noun; רוח יי נוססה בו (the spirit of YHWH lifts up a banner in him) is from the root of נס (banner). And this meaning is close to the words of Daniel: 'and there shall be a time of distress for all the world such as has never been' (Daniel 12:1), and Israel alone shall be delivered; therefore after this, 'a redeemer shall come to Zion.'"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 59, :notes {2 "On 'נוססה': translated hyper-literally as a hophal of נסס from נס (banner); the form is unusual and the sense 'lift up a banner' follows Ibn Ezra's explicit gloss 'מגזרת נס.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:00:24.425661148Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}